      "completion_tokens": 1155,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more electronegative (it pulls electrons more strongly) than hydrogen, the electrons in the bond are pulled toward the oxygen. This creates a \"dipole,\" giving water a slightly negative charge near the oxygen and a slightly positive charge near the hydrogens. This polarity allows water to stick to itself and other polar substances, which is why it is such a good solvent.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists in all three states of matter:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (when frozen)\n*   **Liquid:** Water (the state we use every day)\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (when evaporated)\n\n### 3. Key Physical Properties\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is essential for biological processes, as it allows nutrients to move through cells.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the human body stable.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body. It is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, clouds, and underground aquifers.\n\nIn short, water is the essential fluid of life, the driver of Earth's climate, and a fundamental building block of the universe."
